{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791728397487,"sessionID":"ses_ed4aa686fffeRBfsdkp4XPbH5d","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_ET_iasZqYI243LomdX7Werb0116","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/image_analysis-human-vs-ai-coding-style/brief.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/image_analysis-human-vs-ai-coding-style/brief.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Brief: analysis: human vs ai coding style\n2: \n3: type: feature\n4: id: image\n5: branch: feature/image_analysis-human-vs-ai-coding-style\n6: date: 2026-10-11\n7: author: Leander Muskalla\n8: \n9: ## What\n10: \n11: Can you do a full analysis of two different components.\n12: One is the whole todo feature which was built by me. Another is time-tracking which was built by AI.\n13: Have a detailed look at the state services, the layout, the page view and all relevant components. Do a in-depth comparison of coding style - what does the human do that the AI doesn't and other way around? How do the styles differ?\n14: Write a full report to docs/coding-style.md.\n15: \n16: ## Why\n17: \n18: <!-- Why does this need to exist? What problem does it solve for the user? -->\n19: \n20: ## Out of scope\n21: \n22: <!-- What are we explicitly NOT doing in this job? -->\n23: \n24: ## Notes\n25: \n26: <!-- Anything the analyst or developer should know before starting. -->\n\n(End of file - total 26 lines)\n</content>\n\n<system-reminder>\nInstructions from: /workspace/docs/AGENTS.md\n# solyto frontend\n\n<!--\nThis is your project context, loaded by the agent at the start of every session.\nmanigot is vendor-agnostic: it runs Claude Code or OpenCode against the same\nproject (`mg --profile claude-pro` vs `mg --profile zai`/`--profile\nopencode-go`), and this one file serves both — manigot mounts it read-only\nwherever the selected tool looks for it\n(/workspace/AGENTS.md for OpenCode, /workspace/.claude/CLAUDE.md for Claude\nCode). Those mount paths are read-only: to change this context, edit this\nfile (docs/AGENTS.md), never the mount paths.\nThe same global agents are available under @name either way, and custom\nproject agents in docs/agents/ work under both tools — write them in the\nbuilt-in format (name:, description:, tools: Read, Grep, ...), no per-tool\nformat needed. To make a custom agent read-only under OpenCode, add a\n`permission:` frontmatter block (the built-in format manigot's conversion\npasses through to OpenCode's schema — see the manigot README's agent section);\nthe read-only built-in agents' blocks deny the destructive git commands\n(worktree management, branch -d/-D, reset, checkout, push, ...).\nCustom agents that must commit (like the built-in developer/reviewer/quality)\ndeclare `commit: true` in their frontmatter; agents that never commit declare\n`commit: false` and get a read-only git mount. The default — no agent named,\nfile missing, or marker absent/unknown — is a writable git mount, so a\ncommitting agent is never broken by a missing marker.\nAgent sessions also restrict git to reading history and making commits (the\nsession git shim): worktree management, branch deletes, resets, checkouts,\npushes, and the other destructive subcommands are refused.\nKeep this file tool-neutral — write it for \"the agent\", not for one vendor.\n-->\n\nThe client for [solyto.app](https://solyto.app): a free, private, all-in-one personal management app — todos, notes, calendar, contacts, media libraries, check-in, finances, feeds, time tracking, clipboard, and dev-requests in one place. This repo is the frontend only: a SvelteKit 2 / Svelte 5 single-page app that runs on the web, as an installable PWA, or as a native desktop/mobile shell. There is no backend or database here — every piece of data comes from the solyto REST API, which lives in a separate repository.\n\n## Stack\n- Backend: none in this repo — pure client-side SPA. Consumes the solyto REST API at `/api/v1` (`api.solyto.app` in prod, `http://localhost:8000` in dev; configurable per hostname in `src/lib/config/platform.ts`).\n- Frontend: SvelteKit 2, Svelte 5 (runes, `$state`), TypeScript (strict), Vite 7, Tailwind CSS v4 via `@tailwindcss/vite`, adapter-node (outputs to `build/`).\n- Database: none — persistence is owned by the backend. The client only stores auth tokens, theme, and preferences in LocalStorage (`src/lib/services/LocalStorageService.ts`).\n- Key packages: `@tiptap/*` (notes rich-text editor), `echarts` (statistics charts), `@simplewebauthn/browser` (passkey login), `marked` + `dompurify` (markdown), `@lucide/svelte` (icons), `svelte-dnd-action` / `svelte-gestures` (drag & drop, swipe), `canvas-confetti`, `@fontsource/*`, `@vvo/tzdb`.\n\n## Architecture\n- `src/routes/` — SvelteKit file-based routes, all client-rendered (no `+page.server.ts`/`+server.ts` anywhere). The root `+layout.svelte` bootstraps every state store via `setContext`, guards unauthenticated access (redirect to `/auth/login` or `/setup`), and renders the navbar/footer shells. Feature routes: `/todos`, `/notes`, `/calendar`, `/contacts`, `/check-in`, `/finances`, `/feeds`, `/time-tracking`, `/clipboard`, `/dev-requests`, `/libraries/{books,movies,music,games,links,quotes,recipes,plants}`, plus `/settings`, `/admin`, `/profile`, `/auth/*`, `/setup`, `/share`.\n- `src/lib/state/` — one Svelte 5 runes class per domain (`Auth`, `Todos`, `Notes`, `Calendars`, `BookLibrary`, ...). Instances are created in `+layout.svelte` and shared through Svelte context (`setContext`/`getContext`); each store fetches via `ApiService` and holds the result in reactive `$state`.\n- `src/lib/services/` — `ApiService` (fetch wrapper over the REST API: Bearer auth, JSON, `%s`/`%d` URL placeholders, FormData uploads) and pure domain services (`TodoFilterService`, `TodoSortingService`, `TodoGroupingService`, `TodoRelevanceService`, `LibraryFilterService`, `WealthStatisticsService`, ...) that are unit-tested.\n- `src/lib/config/` — `apiRoutes.ts` (all endpoints, composed from `getApiUrl()` + `/api/v1`), `platform.ts` (web vs. desktop vs. mobile via `PUBLIC_DESKTOP`/`PUBLIC_MOBILE`, API URL resolution), `themes.ts`, `navigation.ts`, `urls.ts`, `features.ts`.\n- `src/lib/components/` — feature components plus shared `ui/` (buttons, modals, navbar), `forms/` (inputs, pickers, toggles), and `charts/` (echarts wrappers). Several features keep separate desktop/mobile view variants (e.g. `calendars/views/{day,week,month,list}`, `todos/views/`).\n- `src/lib/types/` — TypeScript interfaces mirroring the API data model, one file per domain (`todo.ts`, `note.ts`, `library_*.ts`, `calendar.ts`, ...).\n- `src/lib/i18n/` — translation tables `en.ts`, `de.ts`, `es.ts`, `fr.ts`, typed via `types/translation.ts`; every UI string must exist in all four.\n- `src/lib/helpers/` — pure utilities (DateHelper, NumberHelper, FormatHelper, ColorHelper, MusicTheory, ...).\n- `src/service-worker.ts` — PWA service worker (app-shell precache, stale-while-revalidate, push notifications); `static/site.webmanifest` is the PWA manifest, `static/themes/*` are runtime-loaded theme CSS.\n- `tests/` — Vitest unit tests for pure helpers/services only (no component or integration tests); run in node env with `TZ=UTC` and `$lib` aliased to `src/lib`.\n- Data flow: component → state store → `ApiService` → REST API. There is no server-side rendering of app data.\n\n## Coding policies\nWhen writing or reviewing code, follow the policy set in `docs/` — the rules are grounded in this repo's actual idioms, and AI agents should treat them as binding:\n- `docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md` — general in-file principles (readability, DRY, early returns, naming, comments, `$derived`, tooling)\n- `docs/STATE_SERVICES.md` — the `src/lib/state/*.svelte.ts` layer\n- `docs/COMPONENTS.md` — the `src/lib/components/**` and `src/routes/**` UI layer\n- `docs/DATA_LAYER.md` — `services/`, `helpers/`, `config/`, `types/`, `i18n/` and the data flow\n- `docs/TESTING.md` — the test coverage plan (\"test logic and contracts, not markup, not third-party internals\")\n\n## Commands\n- `npm run dev` — start the Vite dev server on port 5173 (expects the API at `http://localhost:8000`)\n- `npm run build` — production build via `vite build` (adapter-node, output in `build/`)\n- `npm run preview` — preview the production build locally\n- `npm run check` — type-check with `svelte-check` (a11y warnings selectively ignored)\n- `npm run lint` — `prettier --check .` + `eslint .`\n- `npm run format` — auto-format with `prettier --write .`\n- `npm test` / `npm run test:watch` — run Vitest unit tests / watch mode\n- `make deploy` / `make audit` — Ansible deploy (external `deployment/` repo) / `npm audit` + `npm outdated`\n\n## Hard rules\n- NEVER modify files outside /workspace\n- NEVER install packages without asking (dependencies are pinned, `engine-strict=true`)\n- NEVER commit secrets: auth tokens, API keys, or `.env*` files (gitignored); never log or print token or user data\n- NEVER hand-edit generated output — `build/` and `.svelte-kit/` are regenerated by `npm run build` / `npm run check`\n- When adding or changing UI strings, keep all four i18n files (`en`, `de`, `es`, `fr`) in sync — `npm run check` enforces it\n- When scope is unclear: ask, don't guess\n- Do not refactor things unrelated to the current task\n- Do not add abstractions not already present in the codebase\n\n</system-reminder>","metadata":{"preview":"# Brief: analysis: human vs ai coding style\n\ntype: feature\nid: image\nbranch: feature/image_analysis-human-vs-ai-coding-style\ndate: 2026-10-11\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nCan you do a full analysis of two different components.\nOne is the whole todo feature which was built by me. Another is time-tracking which was built by AI.\nHave a detailed look at the state services, the layout, the page view and all relevant components. Do a in-depth comparison of coding style - what does the human do that the AI doesn't and other way around? How do the styles differ?\nWrite a full report to docs/coding-style.md.\n\n## Why\n\n<!-- Why does this need to exist? What problem does it solve for the user? -->\n\n## Out of scope","truncated":false,"loaded":["/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md"],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/jobs/image_analysis-human-vs-ai-coding-style/brief.md","text":"# Brief: analysis: human vs ai coding style\n\ntype: feature\nid: image\nbranch: feature/image_analysis-human-vs-ai-coding-style\ndate: 2026-10-11\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nCan you do a full analysis of two different components.\nOne is the whole todo feature which was built by me. Another is time-tracking which was built by AI.\nHave a detailed look at the state services, the layout, the page view and all relevant components. Do a in-depth comparison of coding style - what does the human do that the AI doesn't and other way around? How do the styles differ?\nWrite a full report to docs/coding-style.md.\n\n## Why\n\n<!-- Why does this need to exist? What problem does it solve for the user? -->\n\n## Out of scope\n\n<!-- What are we explicitly NOT doing in this job? -->\n\n## Notes\n\n<!-- Anything the analyst or developer should know before starting. -->","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":26,"totalLines":26,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/jobs/image_analysis-human-vs-ai-coding-style/brief.md","time":{"start":1791728397320,"end":1791728397462}},"id":"prt_12b55abde001zUP5D1qUs2KK1Z","sessionID":"ses_ed4aa686fffeRBfsdkp4XPbH5d","messageID":"msg_12b559c89001r0cXqGfZlPD5NG"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791728397488,"sessionID":"ses_ed4aa686fffeRBfsdkp4XPbH5d","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_ET_kJCh0x93Fz9hXRt8XU0l8290","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/image_analysis-human-vs-ai-coding-style/tasks.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/image_analysis-human-vs-ai-coding-style/tasks.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Tasks: analysis: human vs ai coding style\n2: \n3: id: image\n4: analyst:\n5: date: 2026-10-11\n6: \n7: <!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\n8: \n9: ## Task breakdown\n10: \n11: This is a documentation/analysis job: the deliverable is a single report file\n12: `docs/coding-style.md` comparing the human-built todos feature against the\n13: AI-built time-tracking feature. No source code may be modified. The two\n14: feature stacks are the \"read-only inputs\" listed under each task; the only\n15: file that may be created/changed is the report.\n16: \n17: Ground truth for the comparison is the repo's own conventions in\n18: `docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md`, `docs/STATE_SERVICES.md`, `docs/COMPONENTS.md`,\n19: `docs/DATA_LAYER.md`, `docs/TESTING.md` — they were written for this\n20: codebase and both features should be measured against them, not against\n21: generic taste. Objectively verifiable metrics (file/line references, LOC,\n22: component/test counts) are essential so every \"human does X / AI does Y\"\n23: claim is checkable.\n24: \n25: TASK-1: Inventory the human-built todos feature end to end (state store, 4 pure services, types, routes, views, nav, props, actions) and collect stylistic observations with concrete file/line references.\n26:      files (read-only): src/lib/state/Todos.svelte.ts, src/lib/services/Todo{Services}/*.ts, src/lib/types/todo.ts, src/routes/todos/{+layout,+page}.svelte, src/lib/components/todos/** (views/, props/, actions/, TodoNavigation*, ListEntry, ScoreBar, Counter, NoTodos, ViewSwitcher)\n27:      depends: none\n28:      risk: low — pure reading; only risk is incompleteness, mitigated by enumerating the full component list.\n29: \n30: TASK-2: Inventory the AI-built time-tracking feature end to end (state store, grouping service, types, layout/pages incl. [id], nav, widgets, entry components) and collect stylistic observations with concrete file/line references.\n31:      files (read-only): src/lib/state/TimeTracking.svelte.ts, src/lib/services/TimeTrackingGroupingService.ts, src/lib/types/time_tracking.ts, src/routes/time-tracking/{+layout,+page,[id]/+page}.svelte, src/lib/components/time-tracking/**\n32:      depends: none\n33:      risk: low — pure reading; same completeness caveat as TASK-1.\n34: \n35: TASK-3: Compare both features layer by layer (state service shape, layout vs page responsibilities, view/component decomposition, service extraction, types, reactivity patterns, error handling, comments, helpers reuse, i18n usage, tests) and derive the concrete style-difference list — what the human does that the AI doesn't, and vice versa.\n36:      files: none written in this task; findings feed TASK-5 (docs/coding-style.md)\n37:      depends: TASK-1, TASK-2\n38:      risk: medium — subjective claims need to be grounded in file/line refs and objective metrics (counts, LOC) to be credible and avoid rework.\n39: \n40: TASK-4: Cross-check both codebases against the repo's documented conventions (docs/BEST_PRACTICES, STATE_SERVICES, COMPONENTS, DATA_LAYER, TESTING) and classify each observation as \"matches repo idiom\" vs \"deviates\", separating human-vs-AI differences from idiomatic-vs-non-idiomatic ones (note: the docs themselves are human-authored, so human deviations from them are noteworthy too).\n41:      files (read-only): docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md, docs/STATE_SERVICES.md, docs/COMPONENTS.md, docs/DATA_LAYER.md, docs/TESTING.md\n42:      depends: TASK-1, TASK-2\n43:      risk: low — no code touched; risk is misquoting the policy docs, mitigated by quoting section headers/paraphrasing carefully.\n44: \n45: TASK-5: Write the full report to docs/coding-style.md — methodology, per-layer comparison, a style-characteristics table, \"what the human does that the AI doesn't\" and the reverse, concrete examples with file/line references, notes on test coverage (tests/unit/stores/{Todos,TimeTracking}.test.ts, tests/unit/services/*), and a summary of how the styles differ.\n46:      files: docs/coding-style.md (new file, the only file created; must not modify any src/** or tests/** file)\n47:      depends: TASK-3, TASK-4\n48:      risk: medium — the report is the whole deliverable; length and quality determine whether review passes, so keep claims verifiable and reference real file paths.\n49: \n50: TASK-6: Verify the report — re-read docs/coding-style.md, confirm every referenced file path and line range exists, confirm no source/test/i18n file was changed (report is purely additive), and run `npm run check` as a sanity pass to show the repo still type-checks.\n51:      files: docs/coding-style.md\n52:      depends: TASK-5\n53:      risk: low — no code changes expected; catches stale references and accidental modifications.\n\n(End of file - total 53 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Tasks: analysis: human vs ai coding style\n\nid: image\nanalyst:\ndate: 2026-10-11\n\n<!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\n\n## Task breakdown\n\nThis is a documentation/analysis job: the deliverable is a single report file\n`docs/coding-style.md` comparing the human-built todos feature against the\nAI-built time-tracking feature. No source code may be modified. The two\nfeature stacks are the \"read-only inputs\" listed under each task; the only\nfile that may be created/changed is the report.\n\nGround truth for the comparison is the repo's own conventions in\n`docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md`, `docs/STATE_SERVICES.md`, `docs/COMPONENTS.md`,\n`docs/DATA_LAYER.md`, `docs/TESTING.md` — they were written for this\ncodebase and both features should be measured against them, not against","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/jobs/image_analysis-human-vs-ai-coding-style/tasks.md","text":"# Tasks: analysis: human vs ai coding style\n\nid: image\nanalyst:\ndate: 2026-10-11\n\n<!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\n\n## Task breakdown\n\nThis is a documentation/analysis job: the deliverable is a single report file\n`docs/coding-style.md` comparing the human-built todos feature against the\nAI-built time-tracking feature. No source code may be modified. The two\nfeature stacks are the \"read-only inputs\" listed under each task; the only\nfile that may be created/changed is the report.\n\nGround truth for the comparison is the repo's own conventions in\n`docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md`, `docs/STATE_SERVICES.md`, `docs/COMPONENTS.md`,\n`docs/DATA_LAYER.md`, `docs/TESTING.md` — they were written for this\ncodebase and both features should be measured against them, not against\ngeneric taste. Objectively verifiable metrics (file/line references, LOC,\ncomponent/test counts) are essential so every \"human does X / AI does Y\"\nclaim is checkable.\n\nTASK-1: Inventory the human-built todos feature end to end (state store, 4 pure services, types, routes, views, nav, props, actions) and collect stylistic observations with concrete file/line references.\n     files (read-only): src/lib/state/Todos.svelte.ts, src/lib/services/Todo{Services}/*.ts, src/lib/types/todo.ts, src/routes/todos/{+layout,+page}.svelte, src/lib/components/todos/** (views/, props/, actions/, TodoNavigation*, ListEntry, ScoreBar, Counter, NoTodos, ViewSwitcher)\n     depends: none\n     risk: low — pure reading; only risk is incompleteness, mitigated by enumerating the full component list.\n\nTASK-2: Inventory the AI-built time-tracking feature end to end (state store, grouping service, types, layout/pages incl. [id], nav, widgets, entry components) and collect stylistic observations with concrete file/line references.\n     files (read-only): src/lib/state/TimeTracking.svelte.ts, src/lib/services/TimeTrackingGroupingService.ts, src/lib/types/time_tracking.ts, src/routes/time-tracking/{+layout,+page,[id]/+page}.svelte, src/lib/components/time-tracking/**\n     depends: none\n     risk: low — pure reading; same completeness caveat as TASK-1.\n\nTASK-3: Compare both features layer by layer (state service shape, layout vs page responsibilities, view/component decomposition, service extraction, types, reactivity patterns, error handling, comments, helpers reuse, i18n usage, tests) and derive the concrete style-difference list — what the human does that the AI doesn't, and vice versa.\n     files: none written in this task; findings feed TASK-5 (docs/coding-style.md)\n     depends: TASK-1, TASK-2\n     risk: medium — subjective claims need to be grounded in file/line refs and objective metrics (counts, LOC) to be credible and avoid rework.\n\nTASK-4: Cross-check both codebases against the repo's documented conventions (docs/BEST_PRACTICES, STATE_SERVICES, COMPONENTS, DATA_LAYER, TESTING) and classify each observation as \"matches repo idiom\" vs \"deviates\", separating human-vs-AI differences from idiomatic-vs-non-idiomatic ones (note: the docs themselves are human-authored, so human deviations from them are noteworthy too).\n     files (read-only): docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md, docs/STATE_SERVICES.md, docs/COMPONENTS.md, docs/DATA_LAYER.md, docs/TESTING.md\n     depends: TASK-1, TASK-2\n     risk: low — no code touched; risk is misquoting the policy docs, mitigated by quoting section headers/paraphrasing carefully.\n\nTASK-5: Write the full report to docs/coding-style.md — methodology, per-layer comparison, a style-characteristics table, \"what the human does that the AI doesn't\" and the reverse, concrete examples with file/line references, notes on test coverage (tests/unit/stores/{Todos,TimeTracking}.test.ts, tests/unit/services/*), and a summary of how the styles differ.\n     files: docs/coding-style.md (new file, the only file created; must not modify any src/** or tests/** file)\n     depends: TASK-3, TASK-4\n     risk: medium — the report is the whole deliverable; length and quality determine whether review passes, so keep claims verifiable and reference real file paths.\n\nTASK-6: Verify the report — re-read docs/coding-style.md, confirm every referenced file path and line range exists, confirm no source/test/i18n file was changed (report is purely additive), and run `npm run check` as a sanity pass to show the repo still type-checks.\n     files: docs/coding-style.md\n     depends: TASK-5\n     risk: low — no code changes expected; catches stale references and accidental modifications.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":53,"totalLines":53,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/jobs/image_analysis-human-vs-ai-coding-style/tasks.md","time":{"start":1791728397360,"end":1791728397475}},"id":"prt_12b55ac0d001FQzjZZfPkHmwWU","sessionID":"ses_ed4aa686fffeRBfsdkp4XPbH5d","messageID":"msg_12b559c89001r0cXqGfZlPD5NG"}}
