{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791728734289,"sessionID":"ses_ed4a56c6fffevAGmwB1j5o7wcj","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_vtLB1hKSNniaItPnmizk5868","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/coding-style.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/coding-style.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Human vs. AI coding style — a two-feature comparison\n2: \n3: This report compares two feature stacks in the solyto frontend written by\n4: different authors:\n5: \n6: - **`todos`** — built and evolved by hand (the repo owner) over many commits.\n7: - **`time-tracking`** — built by an AI coding agent (`deepseek-v4-flash` via the\n8:   opencode-go profile), most recently reworked in the archived job\n9:   `docs/jobs/archive/joke_time-tracking-improvements/` (see its\n10:   `implementation.md` header `developer: deepseek-v4-flash (opencode-go)` and\n11:   `verdict.md`).\n12: \n13: The question the brief asks is deceptively simple: *what does the human do that\n14: the AI doesn't, and vice versa?* This report answers it with concrete,\n15: checkable evidence — file paths, line numbers, and objective counts — rather\n16: than taste.\n17: \n18: The two features are not the same size or maturity, so the point is **not**\n19: \"which is better\". It is to characterise the two styles and to see which one\n20: tracks the repo's own written conventions (`docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md`,\n21: `STATE_SERVICES.md`, `COMPONENTS.md`, `DATA_LAYER.md`, `TESTING.md`).\n22: Surprisingly often, the answers are not the ones a reader would guess.\n23: \n24: ---\n25: \n26: ## 1. Methodology\n27: \n28: - **Read-only.** Nothing under `src/**` or `tests/**` is modified by this job;\n29:   the only artifact produced is this report.\n30: - **Ground truth is the repo's own policies**, not generic Svelte taste. The\n31:   five policy documents under `docs/` were written for this codebase and both\n32:   features are measured against them.\n33: - **Objective metrics** (line counts, file counts, test counts, i18n key\n34:   counts, `$derived` usage, comment counts) were collected directly with\n35:   `wc`/`grep`/`git`; every number in the tables below is reproducible.\n36: - **Authorship.** Both features commit under the same git author identity, so\n37:   authorship is established from the brief plus the archived AI job, not from\n38:   `git blame`. Time-tracking's history is dominated by one large AI commit\n39:   (`b25801c \"time tracking improvements\"`, 33 files, +2441/−579) plus a small\n40:   follow-up (`48cea28`); todos accumulated ~33 separate commits over months.\n41: \n42: ### Metrics snapshot\n43: \n44: | Metric (feature source)                    |                      todos (human) |                 time-tracking (AI) |\n45: | ------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------: | ---------------------------------: |\n46: | State store                                |       `Todos.svelte.ts` — 523 LOC |   `TimeTracking.svelte.ts` — 171 LOC |\n47: | Pure domain services                       | 4 files — 249 LOC (`Todo*Service`) | 1 file — 46 LOC (`TimeTrackingGroupingService`) |\n48: | Types                                      |             `todo.ts` — 133 LOC |       `time_tracking.ts` — 97 LOC |\n49: | Components                                 |             35 files — 2848 LOC |              11 files — 1517 LOC |\n50: | Routes (`+layout`/`+page`/`[id]`)          |                2 files — 96 LOC |                3 files — 301 LOC |\n51: | **Feature source total**                   |                       **3849 LOC** |                       **2132 LOC** |\n52: | Store methods (async / total)              |                         31 / ~48 |                           16 / ~19 |\n53: | Component tests                            | Counter, NoTodos (via `logic-leaves`), `context-harness` | none |\n54: | Store/service test LOC                     |    ~933 (store 367 + services 540 + component) |            160 (store 64 + service 96) |\n55: | Test : source ratio (approx.)              |                             ~0.24 |                              ~0.075 |\n56: | i18n keys in feature block (`en.ts`)       |                     61 (`todos`) |           54 (`timeTracking`) |\n57: | Why-comments in feature source             |                  ~0 in components |                        8 comment lines |\n58: | `aria-label` usage in feature components   |                          0 files |                          2 files |\n59: | Dead code (unreferenced component LOC)     |                 0 (`FindCategoryMenu` is used by `settings/WorkspaceEdit`) |      **211 LOC** (`PomodoroWidget.svelte`) |\n60: \n61: Counts are of the feature's own source (state + services + types + components +\n62: routes) and its own tests; shared infra (ApiService, i18n, harnesses) is\n63: excluded. Reproduce with `wc -l` / `grep -rc`.\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: ## 2. State service shape\n68: \n69: This is the layer with the clearest, most policy-relevant differences.\n70: \n71: ### 2.1 Dependency injection\n72: \n73: `docs/STATE_SERVICES.md` names `Todos` as *the reference* for constructor\n74: dependency injection and marks the field-initialiser style (citing `Clipboard`)\n75: as the \"older — not the target style\".\n76: \n77: - **Human (`todos`)** follows the preferred shape exactly:\n78:   `Todos.svelte.ts:62-72` injects `auth`, `apiService` and `localStorage` with\n79:   defaults that preserve `new Todos()`.\n80: - **AI (`time-tracking`)** uses the older field-initialiser shape:\n81:   `TimeTracking.svelte.ts:26-31` declares `auth = getAuth();` and assigns\n82:   `this.apiService = this.auth.apiService` in the constructor. It defaults to\n83:   the shared `auth.apiService` (good), but it cannot be overridden by a\n84:   constructor argument and forces tests to mock the `Auth` module.\n85: \n86: This is the one place where the human is *directly* the textbook example the\n87: policy cites, and the AI is the cited anti-pattern.\n88: \n89: ### 2.2 Member ordering\n90: \n91: `docs/STATE_SERVICES.md` §\"Member ordering\" says `Todos` is the reference order:\n92: constants → `$state` fields → collaborators → constructor → public methods →\n93: `Symbol` + `setX`/`getX` at the bottom.\n94: \n95: - Both stores follow the order well. `TimeTracking.svelte.ts` has no `static\n96:   readonly` constants, no `Symbol`-adjacent deviations, and puts\n97:   `setTimeTracking`/`getTimeTracking` at the bottom (`:163-171`) — compliant.\n98: - The AI's store is noticeably **flatter and more consistent** in its CRUD\n99:   bodies: every mutator is `const res = await …; if (res) await this.load…;\n100:   return res;` (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:60-153`). The human's store mixes\n101:   `return res;`, `return ok;` and `return Promise.resolve(res !== null);` in the\n102:   same file (`Todos.svelte.ts:280-317` vs `:331-359`), including\n103:   `return Promise.resolve(...)` on already-`async` methods — redundant wrapping\n104:   that the AI never does.\n105: \n106: ### 2.3 Delegation to pure services\n107: \n108: `docs/STATE_SERVICES.md` §\"Delegate pure logic to `services/`\" calls out `Todos`\n109: as the model: it holds four service instances as fields\n110: (`Todos.svelte.ts:57-60`) and delegates filtering, sorting, grouping and scoring.\n111: \n112: - **Human:** 4 single-purpose services (`TodoFilterService`,\n113:   `TodoSortingService`, `TodoGroupingService`, `TodoRelevanceService`), each\n114:   `new`-able, pure, and unit-tested.\n115: - **AI:** 1 service (`TimeTrackingGroupingService`) with a good \"why\" docblock\n116:   (`:13-17`). But other pure logic that the policy says should live in a service\n117:   is left inline in components:\n118:   - **`groupByDate` is duplicated verbatim** in\n119:     `GroupedEntries.svelte:18-30` and `routes/time-tracking/[id]/+page.svelte:67-79`.\n120:   - Week/today boundary math and aggregation live inline inside `$derived`\n121:     closures in `DashboardView.svelte:24-46`.\n122:   - `formatDuration` — pure string formatting — lives on the store\n123:     (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:155-160`), which `docs/DATA_LAYER.md` would place\n124:     in `helpers/` (there is a `NumberHelper`/`FormatHelper` family for this).\n125: \n126: The AI clearly *can* extract a service (it did, and tested it well); it just\n127: doesn't do so as consistently as the human.\n128: \n129: ### 2.4 `load()` + `loaded` and error handling\n130: \n131: `docs/STATE_SERVICES.md` §\"The `load()` + `loaded` convention\" wants sub-loads\n132: via `Promise.all` and a single `if (res)` assignment; `docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md`\n133: §\"Early returns\" and the general robustness rules favour explicit handling.\n134: \n135: - **Human:** `Todos.load()` (`:74-97`) sequences named steps and wraps the\n136:   parallel sub-loads in `try { … } finally { this.loadHideIt(); }` (`:92-96`),\n137:   and `loadWorkspaces` has a retry parameter (`:107-118`). Defensive,\n138:   failure-aware, and the reason its store test file is 367 lines.\n139: - **AI:** `TimeTracking.load()` (`:33-40`) is a bare `await Promise.all([…])`\n140:   followed by `this.loaded = true` — no `try`/`catch`/`finally`, no retry, no\n141:   guard if one sub-load rejects. Simpler and readable, but less robust.\n142: \n143: ### 2.5 UI state on the store\n144: \n145: `docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"Local UI state via `$state`\" says \"UI-only state stays in\n146: the component; anything the route, another component or the backend cares about\n147: belongs on a store.\"\n148: \n149: - **Human:** the store carries transient UI flags — `quickCreateOpen`\n150:   (`Todos.svelte.ts:53`, written by `TodoCreate.svelte:62,73` and read by\n151:   `ViewSwitcher.svelte:10`) and `recentlyCreated` (`:52`, a 10-second highlight\n152:   set by `quickCreate`). `sortByScore`/`hideItActive` on the store are defensible\n153:   (shared/persisted), but `quickCreateOpen` is arguably a component concern.\n154: - **AI:** keeps every modal/drawer flag in the component that owns it —\n155:   `showEntryCreate` (`routes/time-tracking/+page.svelte:13`), `editModal` /\n156:   `deleteModal` / `editingEntry` (`[id]/+page.svelte:32-35`),\n157:   `showProjectCreate` (`+layout.svelte:15`). Cleaner separation.\n158: \n159: Here the AI is the more policy-compliant one.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 3. Layout vs. page responsibilities\n164: \n165: `docs/DATA_LAYER.md` says each feature layout \"creates its feature stores **and\n166: calls `load()` on them**\".\n167: \n168: - **Human:** `routes/todos/+layout.svelte:1-11` is minimal — it only calls\n169:   `setTodos()`/`setTags()`; the actual bootstrap (`onMount` → `todos.load()`,\n170:   viewport defaulting, `tags.load()`) lives in `routes/todos/+page.svelte:28-37`.\n171: - **AI:** `routes/time-tracking/+layout.svelte:10-27` does\n172:   `setTimeTracking()`, `onMount` → `tt.load()`, and gates the whole shell behind\n173:   `{#if tt.loaded}` — it also owns the navigation and the create/edit project\n174:   modal orchestration. This matches the documented \"layout bootstraps the\n175:   feature\" pattern more literally.\n176: \n177: Two corollaries:\n178: \n179: 1. **Single responsibility of the layout.** The AI's layout is a full shell\n180:    (nav + load + 2 modals + gate); the human's is a one-liner. The human's\n181:    layout is *simpler*, the AI's is *closer to the documented intent*. Both are\n182:    defensible; they are simply different axes.\n183: 2. **Nested route.** Time-tracking has a real detail route\n184:    (`[id]/+page.svelte`, 205 LOC) and derives the selected project from\n185:    `page.params.id` (`+layout.svelte:18`, `[id]/+page.svelte:37-39`). Todos has\n186:    no detail route — selection is pure store state (`activeWorkspace`,\n187:    `activeFilters`). The AI's route-driven selection survived reloads and deep\n188:    links; the human's filter state is rebuilt from `page.url.searchParams` in\n189:    `checkUrlForFilter()` (`Todos.svelte.ts:148-159`).\n190: \n191: ---\n192: \n193: ## 4. View / component decomposition\n194: \n195: - **Human (`todos`)** decomposes along *view mode* and *prop*: four view\n196:   variants (`views/{ListView,CardView,OverviewView,KanbanView}`), a per-field\n197:   `props/` folder (15 tiny components: `Title`, `Category`, `Priority`, …), an\n198:   `actions/` folder (`TodoCreate`, `TodoDelete`, `HideIt`, `SortByScore`) and a\n199:   desktop/mobile navigation split (`TodoNavigationDesktop` 149 LOC /\n200:   `TodoNavigationMobile` 216 LOC). Components are small and single-purpose, at\n201:   the cost of 35 files and some duplication (`getBgColor` is copy-pasted in\n202:   `Card.svelte:31-40` and `CompactCard.svelte:15-24` — allowed by the \"three or\n203:   more times\" DRY rule).\n204: - **AI (`time-tracking`)** decomposes along *screen/role*: one navigation\n205:   component (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte`, 363 LOC — the largest file in\n206:   either feature), one entry component, one entry editor, one project editor,\n207:   and one component per dashboard widget. Views are selected by a URL `?tab=`\n208:   param (`routes/time-tracking/+page.svelte:15-19`,\n209:   `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:31-35`) rather than a component switcher.\n210: \n211: The AI's `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte` concentrates project list, category\n212: CRUD, a colour picker, inline rename, two delete-confirmation modals and the\n213: mobile drawer in one 363-line component. `docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"Keep the\n214: `<script>` readable … If it stops being scannable, split components\" suggests\n215: the human's instinct (split by concern) is the documented one, though the AI\n216: keeps the file logically sectioned with snippets.\n217: \n218: **Dead code:** `PomodoroWidget.svelte` (211 LOC) is imported nowhere in `src/`\n219: (verified with `grep -rl`). It is the single largest dead-code artifact found in\n220: either feature. The human has no equivalent (the one candidate,\n221: `FindCategoryMenu.svelte`, is used by `settings/WorkspaceEdit.svelte`).\n222: \n223: ---\n224: \n225: ## 5. Reactivity patterns (`$derived` discipline)\n226: \n227: `docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md` §\"`$derived` discipline\" is explicit: use `$derived`\n228: only for genuine, side-effect-free derivations read while rendering, and move\n229: `filter().map().sort()` chains into a method or service.\n230: \n231: - **Human:** modest use of `$derived` (7 files, one or two per file), and the one\n232:   aggregation is `OverviewView.svelte:19-42` using the correct **`$derived.by`**\n233:   form. Filtering/sorting/grouping is never a `$derived` — it is `Todos.useFilters`,\n234:   a method (`Todos.svelte.ts:161-196`), exactly as the policy prescribes.\n235: - **AI:** two genuine deviations:\n236:   1. **`$derived(() => …)` misuse** in `DashboardView.svelte:24,35,37,46` and\n237:      `StatisticsView.svelte:52`. In Svelte 5 this makes the derived value *a\n238:      function*, forcing call sites like `weekBreakdown()` (`:58,63`) and\n239:      `categoryBreakdown()` (`:66,67`) to invoke it. It happens to work, but it\n240:      is the classic runes footgun; the correct form is `$derived.by(...)` — which\n241:      the *same AI* uses correctly elsewhere (`EntryCreate.svelte:85`,\n242:      `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:31`, `routes/time-tracking/+page.svelte:15`).\n243:      Inconsistent within a single feature.\n244:   2. **Aggregation chains inside `$derived`** (`StatisticsView.svelte:52-64`\n245:      builds a `Map`, sums minutes and sorts — precisely the \"`filter().map().sort()`\n246:      hidden behind `$derived`\" the policy says to extract).\n247: \n248: - **Reactive time:** the human uses `SvelteDate` from `svelte/reactivity` in the\n249:   filter pipeline (`Todos.svelte.ts:165-171`) so the \"auto-generated due within\n250:   3 days\" window stays reactive; the AI uses plain `new Date()` inside\n251:   `$derived` (`DashboardView.svelte:25,38`), which will not re-evaluate when the\n252:   clock moves.\n253: \n254: `routes/todos/+page.svelte:26` has a harmless redundant alias\n255: (`let view = $derived(todos.view)`), but it is not a violation.\n256: \n257: ---\n258: \n259: ## 6. Loading-indicator discipline\n260: \n261: `docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"Loading feedback\": \"Always `start()` before the await and\n262: `stop()` on **every** exit path.\"\n263: \n264: - **AI (`time-tracking`)** is disciplined. `EntryCreate.onSave`\n265:   (`:118-141`), `ProjectCreate.onSave` (`:47-68`), `TimerWidget.startTimer`\n266:   (`:24-34`) all check validity *before* `loadingIndicator.start()`, or stop on\n267:   every path. No leak was found.\n268: - **Human (`todos`)** has several early returns *after* `start()` with no\n269:   `stop()`, leaking the global spinner:\n270:   - `props/Title.svelte:54-65` — `onsubmit()` calls `start()`, then\n271:     `if (todoTitle === '') return;` (`:59-61`) without stopping.\n272:   - `props/Description.svelte:88-99` — same shape at `:93-95`.\n273:   - `props/Subtask.svelte:61-72` — same shape at `:66-68`.\n274:   - `ListAndAdd.svelte:57-76` — `start()` then `if (value.trim() === '') return;`\n275:     (`:60-62`).\n276:   - (`props/Category.svelte:59-90` gets this right by calling `stop()` before its\n277:     empty-title return — showing the human knows the rule, just doesn't apply it\n278:     uniformly.)\n279: \n280: This is the clearest instance of the AI's mechanical consistency beating the\n281: human's hand-written inconsistency.\n282: \n283: ---\n284: \n285: ## 7. Error handling\n286: \n287: - **Human:** real error surfaces. `Todos.loadWorkspaces` retries once\n288:   (`:107-118`); `Category.svelte:78-86` and `Tags.svelte:73-83` call\n289:   `notifications.error(ts.get.todos.category_error / tag_error)` when a create\n290:   fails; `quickCreate` returns a discriminated `{ ok, recurrenceIgnored }`\n291:   result (`Todos.svelte.ts:249-278`) that `TodoCreate.svelte:86-96` turns into\n292:   success/warning notifications.\n293: - **AI:** close to fire-and-forget. Store mutators discard results and reload\n294:   (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:60-153`); the only user feedback is the modal closing\n295:   unconditionally in `EntryCreate.onSave:133-140` / `ProjectCreate.onSave:52-67`,\n296:   even if the API call failed. There are no `notifications.error(...)` calls in\n297:   the time-tracking feature (verified by grep).\n298: \n299: The human treats the API as fallible; the AI treats \"await returns\" as success.\n300: \n301: ---\n302: \n303: ## 8. Comments\n304: \n305: `docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md` §\"Comments explain why, not what\" cites\n306: `Todos.svelte.ts` as the reference (the `// Restore synchronously …` and\n307: `// Always re-apply hide-it …` blocks) and says not to leave commented-out code.\n308: \n309: The current code contradicts the doc in an interesting way:\n310: \n311: - **`Todos.svelte.ts` contains zero comments.** The two comments the policy\n312:   quotes were introduced in `d7d4d92` and removed in `a34275e` (`git log -S`);\n313:   the reference example is now stale. All 35 todos components and both routes\n314:   also contain **no `//` comments at all**; `ScoreBar.svelte:29` even carries a\n315:   commented-out fragment (`<!-- left[-56px] left-2px -->`), which the policy\n316:   explicitly forbids.\n317: - **The AI's code is where the \"why\" comments actually are**: 8 comment lines in\n318:   the feature, e.g. `EntryCreate.svelte:70` (\"No silent default to the first\n319:   project\"), `:109-111` (why duration-only entries anchor to local noon),\n320:   `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:52-53` (why a concrete hex default for the\n321:   colour picker), and the JSDoc block in `TimeTrackingGroupingService.ts:13-17`\n322:   (the \"entries without a project are ignored\" contract).\n323: \n324: So the *human-authored policy* is better honoured by the *AI-authored code* on\n325: this axis. The human's comments migrated into the store tests instead, where\n326: they are genuinely useful (see §11).\n327: \n328: ---\n329: \n330: ## 9. i18n, accessibility and shared primitives\n331: \n332: ### i18n\n333: \n334: `docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"i18n — `ts.get.*`\": \"Never hardcode a UI string in a\n335: component.\"\n336: \n337: - **AI (`time-tracking`)** is consistently translated: every visible string in\n338:   every component goes through `ts.get.timeTracking.*` (grep for bare English\n339:   literals in the feature returns nothing beyond the `—` placeholder and `×`).\n340: - **Human (`todos`)** has real deviations:\n341:   - `Counter.svelte:8` — literal `{todos.length} Todos` (its own test asserts\n342:     `'2 Todos'`).\n343:   - `KanbanView.svelte:27-40` — `'Backlog'`, `'In Progress'`, `'Waiting'`,\n344:     `'Almost done'`, `'Pending'` hardcoded in `getStatusLabel`.\n345:   - `Recurrence.svelte:116,125,144,150` — `Every`, `occurrence(s)`, `Cancel`,\n346:     `Save` hardcoded.\n347:   - `TodoCreate.svelte:121,131,135,139,143,147,159` — placeholder `Enter todo`\n348:     and the legend `Category / Tag / Due Date / Recurrence / Link`.\n349: \n350: This is a striking reversal of the common assumption that AI output is the one\n351: littered with hardcoded strings.\n352: \n353: ### Accessibility\n354: \n355: `docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"Accessibility\" requires keyboard-reachable clickables and\n356: visible focus.\n357: \n358: - **AI:** uses native `<button>` pervasively and gives icon-only controls\n359:   accessible names (`aria-label={ts.get.timeTracking.*}` in\n360:   `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:184,212,224,245,300,308` and\n361:   `ProjectCreate.svelte:102,113`).\n362: - **Human:** relies on `role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"` on non-native elements in 6\n363:   places, but **no todos component uses `aria-label` at all** — several icon-only\n364:   buttons (e.g. the remove-category button `Category.svelte:121-126`, the\n365:   trash button `DueDate.svelte:51-59`) are unlabelled.\n366: \n367: ### Shared primitives\n368: \n369: Both reuse the shared `ui/` and `forms/` primitives, but the human hand-rolls\n370: more raw inputs where a primitive exists: `Title.svelte:74-82`,\n371: `Link.svelte:80-87` and `Recurrence.svelte:117-123,131-136` use\n372: `<input type=\"text\">`/`<input type=\"number\">` with bespoke classes instead of\n373: `forms/TextInput`/`NumberInput`, which `docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"Prefer the shared\n374: primitives\" calls a smell. The AI's forms are assembled from `ContentModal`,\n375: `TextInput`, `NumberInput`, `DateInput`, `DateTimeInput`, `Select` and\n376: `TextButton` throughout (`EntryCreate.svelte:144-208`, `ProjectCreate.svelte:71-145`).\n377: \n378: ---\n379: \n380: ## 10. Types\n381: \n382: - Both mirror the API in snake_case and keep type-only imports; both are\n383:   policy-compliant.\n384: - **Human:** 133 LOC with a rich set of request/response types\n385:   (`CreateTodoRequest`, `UpdateTodoRequest`, `TodoWorkspace`, `TodoSubtask`, …)\n386:   and filter unions (`TodoFilterType`, `TodoDueAtFilter`). Every declared type is\n387:   used.\n388: - **AI:** 97 LOC. The archived verdict already flagged one **dead type**,\n389:   `TimeTrackingStatisticsRequest` (`time_tracking.ts:74-78`), never referenced by\n390:   `loadStatistics` (which takes inline `from`/`to`/`categoryIds` params). A small\n391:   \"declared for completeness, never wired up\" habit.\n392: \n393: Also note `TimeTracking.loadStatistics` (`:146-153`) **builds a query string by\n394: hand** (`apiRoutes.… + '?' + params.join('&')`). `docs/DATA_LAYER.md` says all\n395: endpoints live in `apiRoutes` and callers should never build URL strings. Query\n396: params aren't modelled in `apiRoutes`, so this is a minor, arguably unavoidable\n397: deviation — but it is still URL construction outside the config layer.\n398: \n399: ---\n400: \n401: ## 11. Tests\n402: \n403: `docs/TESTING.md` names `Todos` as the Priority-1 store (highest logic density)\n404: and both `TodoFilterService` and `TimeTrackingGroupingService` as covered pure\n405: logic.\n406: \n407: - **Human (`todos`)** invests heavily in tests:\n408:   - Store: `tests/unit/stores/Todos.test.ts` — **367 lines**, covering\n409:     `useFilters` (backlog, hide-it, category/tag, auto-generated visibility),\n410:     URL filters, `quickCreate` recurrence-ignore, sort-by-score, hide-it\n411:     persistence/restore, and concurrency (\"respects a toggle issued while `load()`\n412:     is in flight\", `:330-365`).\n413:   - Services: `TodoFilterService.test.ts` (280), `TodoSortingService.test.ts`\n414:     (116), `TodoRelevanceService.test.ts` (96), `TodoGroupingService.test.ts` (48)\n415:     — ~540 lines.\n416:   - Components: `todos/Counter` is tested twice\n417:     (`tests/components/counter.test.ts`, `context-harness.test.ts`) and `NoTodos`\n418:     via `logic-leaves.test.ts`.\n419:   - The tests carry **explanatory \"why\" comments** — e.g. the regression note at\n420:     `Todos.test.ts:111-115` explaining a stale-filter bug. This is the human\n421:     comment style relocated into the test suite, where it is arguably most\n422:     valuable.\n423: - **AI (`time-tracking`)** tests the extracted service well but the feature\n424:   thinly:\n425:   - `tests/unit/services/TimeTrackingGroupingService.test.ts` — 96 lines, 5\n426:     cases including \"own colour, not first category\", fallback colour, ignores\n427:     project-less entries and no input mutation. Good coverage of the service.\n428:   - `tests/unit/stores/TimeTracking.test.ts` — only 64 lines: `load()`,\n429:     no-active-timer, one `startTimer`, and `formatDuration` cases. The store's\n430:     other mutators are untested (defensible — `docs/TESTING.md` §8 says not to\n431:     test boilerplate CRUD — but `loadStatistics` param building, which *is*\n432:     non-trivial, has no test).\n433:   - **No component tests** for any time-tracking widget, despite\n434:     `TimeBreakdownWidget` being a clean props-driven leaf (the exact shape\n435:     `docs/TESTING.md` §8 Priority-2 says to test).\n436: \n437: Test : source ratio is roughly **0.24 (human) vs. 0.075 (AI)**.\n438: \n439: ---\n440: \n441: ## 12. Commit granularity\n442: \n443: Not code style, but part of the observed behaviour:\n444: \n445: - **Human:** `todos` shows ~33 commits over months, including small,\n446:   intent-named fixes (`hide it should apply to backlog`,\n447:   `hide it doesnt always reload`, `Auto-create categories in Todos if\n448:   non-existent`). The feature history reads as a sequence of discovered edge\n449:   cases.\n450: - **AI:** `time-tracking`'s rework landed as one 33-file, +2441/−579 commit\n451:   (`b25801c`) plus a small visual follow-up (`48cea28`). Wide, coherent, and\n452:   decisively big — the opposite of incremental.\n453: \n454: ---\n455: \n456: ## 13. Style-characteristics table\n457: \n458: Legend: ✅ matches the repo's documented convention · ⚠️ deviates.\n459: \n460: | Convention (source doc)                                     | todos (human) | time-tracking (AI) |\n461: | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------ |\n462: | Constructor DI (injected collaborators) — STATE_SERVICES     | ✅ reference  | ⚠️ field-init      |\n463: | One class/file, `setX`/`getX`, member order — STATE_SERVICES | ✅            | ✅                 |\n464: | `load()` + `loaded`, parallel sub-loads — STATE_SERVICES     | ✅ (+retry/`finally`) | ✅ (no error handling) |\n465: | Delegate pure logic to `services/` — STATE_SERVICES/DATA     | ✅ 4 services | ⚠️ 1 service; inline `groupByDate`×2, `formatDuration` on store |\n466: | Return semantics (`res`/`ok`, no redundant `Promise.resolve`) — STATE_SERVICES | ⚠️ mixed | ✅ consistent |\n467: | UI-only state stays in component — COMPONENTS                | ⚠️ `quickCreateOpen`, `recentlyCreated` on store | ✅ |\n468: | `$derived` discipline (`$derived.by`, no chains) — BEST_PRACTICES | ✅ `$derived.by` for aggregation | ⚠️ `$derived(() => …)` ×5, aggregation in derived |\n469: | Comments explain why; no commented-out code — BEST_PRACTICES | ⚠️ none; one commented-out fragment | ✅ 8 why-comments (incl. service docblock) |\n470: | Loading indicator stopped on every path — COMPONENTS         | ⚠️ 4 early-return leaks | ✅ |\n471: | Error handling / user feedback — BEST_PRACTICES              | ✅ retry + notifications | ⚠️ fire-and-forget |\n472: | i18n: no hardcoded UI strings — COMPONENTS/DATA              | ⚠️ Counter, Kanban labels, Recurrence, TodoCreate | ✅ fully translated |\n473: | Accessibility: labels + native elements — COMPONENTS         | ⚠️ no `aria-label`; `role=\"button\"` fallbacks | ✅ `aria-label` on icon buttons, native `<button>` |\n474: | Shared primitives — COMPONENTS                               | ⚠️ hand-rolled inputs | ✅ primitives everywhere |\n475: | Types: no dead types, no hand-built URLs — DATA              | ✅ | ⚠️ dead `TimeTrackingStatisticsRequest`; hand-built query string |\n476: | Tests for logic/contracts — TESTING                          | ✅ deep store + 4 service suites + component tests | ⚠️ good service test, thin store test, no component tests |\n477: | No dead components                                            | ✅ | ⚠️ `PomodoroWidget` (211 LOC) unused |\n478: \n479: Score (informational): both authors are **9 ✅ / 7 ⚠️**. The counts are\n480: identical, but they land on *different* rules — which is the whole story.\n481: \n482: ---\n483: \n484: ## 14. What the human does that the AI doesn't\n485: \n486: 1. **Evolves in small, narrative commits.** `todos` history reads as a chain of\n487:    specific fixes; the AI lands a feature in one wide change.\n488: 2. **Extracts pure logic into multiple dedicated services** (`Todo*Service`)\n489:    before it is strictly necessary, and unit-tests each exhaustively.\n490: 3. **Bakes in defensive behaviour**: retry on a failed sub-load, `try/finally`\n491:    so a post-load step always runs, discriminated results\n492:    (`{ ok, recurrenceIgnored }`) instead of bare booleans.\n493: 4. **Surfaces API failures to the user** via `UiNotifications` error messages\n494:    and treats create/update as fallible.\n495: 5. **Writes the reference DI store** the policy quotes verbatim.\n496: 6. **Runs a much deeper store test suite** (367 lines, incl. concurrency and\n497:    regression cases) and keeps test rationale in comments.\n498: 7. **Uses `SvelteDate`** to keep time-window derivation reactive.\n499: 8. **Splits navigation by viewport** into dedicated desktop/mobile components.\n500: \n501: ## 15. What the AI does that the human doesn't\n502: \n503: 1. **Mechanical consistency in CRUD bodies** — one return shape, no redundant\n504:    `Promise.resolve` wrapping.\n505: 2. **Stops the loading indicator on every exit path** (the human leaks it four\n506:    times).\n507: 3. **Translates every UI string** and **labels every icon-only button**; uses\n508:    native `<button>` over `role=\"button\"` divs.\n509: 4. **Assembles forms from shared primitives** (`ContentModal`, `TextInput`,\n510:    `NumberInput`, `DateInput`, `DateTimeInput`, `Select`) instead of hand-rolled\n511:    inputs.\n512: 5. **Writes \"why\" comments** at the non-obvious points (colour defaults, noon\n513:    anchoring, contract assumptions) — the very behaviour the policy documents\n514:    but the human's current code no longer exhibits.\n515: 6. **Keeps transient UI state in components**, not on the store.\n516: 7. **Drives view selection from the URL** (`?tab=`, `[id]` route), so reload /\n517:    deep-link / back-forward work.\n518: 8. **Uses `$derived.by`** where the derivation is a block (and documents pure\n519:    contracts with JSDoc on the service).\n520: \n521: ## 16. Where each author deviates from the repo's own conventions\n522: \n523: - **Human deviations:** i18n gaps (`Counter`, `KanbanView`, `Recurrence`,\n524:   `TodoCreate`); four spinner leaks; no `aria-label`; hand-rolled inputs;\n525:   mixed return semantics; a commented-out markup fragment; UI flags on the store;\n526:   (and, ironically, the absence of the comments the policy attributes to it).\n527:   Note the policy docs are themselves human-authored, so these are the author\n528:   deviating from his own written rules.\n529: - **AI deviations:** the older field-initialiser store shape the docs mark\n530:   \"not the target style\"; `$derived(() => …)` misuse (inconsistent with its own\n531:   correct `$derived.by` usage); pure logic left inline in components; 211 LOC of\n532:   dead component code; a dead type; a hand-built query string; no error feedback\n533:   on failed mutations; and a thinner test footprint for the feature's own UI.\n534: \n535: Neither style is uniformly \"clean\". The human optimises for **robustness and\n536: incremental understanding**; the AI optimises for **local consistency and\n537: convention coverage**.\n538: \n539: ---\n540: \n541: ## 17. Summary — how the styles differ\n542: \n543: The human writes like someone who has been burned by the failures the code will\n544: eventually hit. His store is the policy's reference implementation: injected\n545: dependencies, pure logic pushed into four tested services, retries and\n546: `finally` blocks, user-visible errors, and a deep regression-oriented test\n547: suite. Its weaknesses are the classic ones of hand-written code — drift over\n548: time (stale doc comments, four leaked spinners, hardcoded strings in the\n549: older components, hand-rolled inputs) and a tendency to keep UI flags on the\n550: store.\n551: \n552: The AI writes like a diligent implementer of the written rules, applied locally\n553: and uniformly, without the accumulated wear of real defects. Its CRUD shape,\n554: loading discipline, i18n coverage, accessibility labelling and use of shared\n555: primitives are consistently better *within each file* than the human's. Its\n556: weaknesses are the classic ones of generated code — a few known runes\n557: footguns (`$derived(() => …)`), logic that should have been extracted left\n558: inline, an unused 211-line widget, a dead type, an unused robustness gap\n559: (no error handling), and a thinner, more \"happy-path\" test footprint.\n560: \n561: Put plainly: **the human's code knows failure modes; the AI's code knows the\n562: style guide.** The gap that matters for this repo is not raw capability — both\n563: produce working, readable code — but *where the effort is spent*: the human\n564: spends it on edge cases and structure, the AI on uniformity and surface\n565: conventions. The policy documents (`docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md` et al.) are, on\n566: balance, followed at least as well by the AI on the axes they measure (i18n,\n567: a11y, primitives, loading discipline, comments), and better followed by the\n568: human on the structural and robustness axes (DI, service extraction, error\n569: handling, tests, incremental change).\n570: \n571: A practical reading for future work: the AI's habits are excellent for the\n572: shallow, greppable rules and should be kept; the human's habits are the ones to\n573: copy for the structural, defensive, and testing decisions that the written\n574: policy only gestures at.\n\n(End of file - total 574 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Human vs. AI coding style — a two-feature comparison\n\nThis report compares two feature stacks in the solyto frontend written by\ndifferent authors:\n\n- **`todos`** — built and evolved by hand (the repo owner) over many commits.\n- **`time-tracking`** — built by an AI coding agent (`deepseek-v4-flash` via the\n  opencode-go profile), most recently reworked in the archived job\n  `docs/jobs/archive/joke_time-tracking-improvements/` (see its\n  `implementation.md` header `developer: deepseek-v4-flash (opencode-go)` and\n  `verdict.md`).\n\nThe question the brief asks is deceptively simple: *what does the human do that\nthe AI doesn't, and vice versa?* This report answers it with concrete,\ncheckable evidence — file paths, line numbers, and objective counts — rather\nthan taste.\n\nThe two features are not the same size or maturity, so the point is **not**\n\"which is better\". It is to characterise the two styles and to see which one\ntracks the repo's own written conventions (`docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md`,","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/coding-style.md","text":"# Human vs. AI coding style — a two-feature comparison\n\nThis report compares two feature stacks in the solyto frontend written by\ndifferent authors:\n\n- **`todos`** — built and evolved by hand (the repo owner) over many commits.\n- **`time-tracking`** — built by an AI coding agent (`deepseek-v4-flash` via the\n  opencode-go profile), most recently reworked in the archived job\n  `docs/jobs/archive/joke_time-tracking-improvements/` (see its\n  `implementation.md` header `developer: deepseek-v4-flash (opencode-go)` and\n  `verdict.md`).\n\nThe question the brief asks is deceptively simple: *what does the human do that\nthe AI doesn't, and vice versa?* This report answers it with concrete,\ncheckable evidence — file paths, line numbers, and objective counts — rather\nthan taste.\n\nThe two features are not the same size or maturity, so the point is **not**\n\"which is better\". It is to characterise the two styles and to see which one\ntracks the repo's own written conventions (`docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md`,\n`STATE_SERVICES.md`, `COMPONENTS.md`, `DATA_LAYER.md`, `TESTING.md`).\nSurprisingly often, the answers are not the ones a reader would guess.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Methodology\n\n- **Read-only.** Nothing under `src/**` or `tests/**` is modified by this job;\n  the only artifact produced is this report.\n- **Ground truth is the repo's own policies**, not generic Svelte taste. The\n  five policy documents under `docs/` were written for this codebase and both\n  features are measured against them.\n- **Objective metrics** (line counts, file counts, test counts, i18n key\n  counts, `$derived` usage, comment counts) were collected directly with\n  `wc`/`grep`/`git`; every number in the tables below is reproducible.\n- **Authorship.** Both features commit under the same git author identity, so\n  authorship is established from the brief plus the archived AI job, not from\n  `git blame`. Time-tracking's history is dominated by one large AI commit\n  (`b25801c \"time tracking improvements\"`, 33 files, +2441/−579) plus a small\n  follow-up (`48cea28`); todos accumulated ~33 separate commits over months.\n\n### Metrics snapshot\n\n| Metric (feature source)                    |                      todos (human) |                 time-tracking (AI) |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------: | ---------------------------------: |\n| State store                                |       `Todos.svelte.ts` — 523 LOC |   `TimeTracking.svelte.ts` — 171 LOC |\n| Pure domain services                       | 4 files — 249 LOC (`Todo*Service`) | 1 file — 46 LOC (`TimeTrackingGroupingService`) |\n| Types                                      |             `todo.ts` — 133 LOC |       `time_tracking.ts` — 97 LOC |\n| Components                                 |             35 files — 2848 LOC |              11 files — 1517 LOC |\n| Routes (`+layout`/`+page`/`[id]`)          |                2 files — 96 LOC |                3 files — 301 LOC |\n| **Feature source total**                   |                       **3849 LOC** |                       **2132 LOC** |\n| Store methods (async / total)              |                         31 / ~48 |                           16 / ~19 |\n| Component tests                            | Counter, NoTodos (via `logic-leaves`), `context-harness` | none |\n| Store/service test LOC                     |    ~933 (store 367 + services 540 + component) |            160 (store 64 + service 96) |\n| Test : source ratio (approx.)              |                             ~0.24 |                              ~0.075 |\n| i18n keys in feature block (`en.ts`)       |                     61 (`todos`) |           54 (`timeTracking`) |\n| Why-comments in feature source             |                  ~0 in components |                        8 comment lines |\n| `aria-label` usage in feature components   |                          0 files |                          2 files |\n| Dead code (unreferenced component LOC)     |                 0 (`FindCategoryMenu` is used by `settings/WorkspaceEdit`) |      **211 LOC** (`PomodoroWidget.svelte`) |\n\nCounts are of the feature's own source (state + services + types + components +\nroutes) and its own tests; shared infra (ApiService, i18n, harnesses) is\nexcluded. Reproduce with `wc -l` / `grep -rc`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. State service shape\n\nThis is the layer with the clearest, most policy-relevant differences.\n\n### 2.1 Dependency injection\n\n`docs/STATE_SERVICES.md` names `Todos` as *the reference* for constructor\ndependency injection and marks the field-initialiser style (citing `Clipboard`)\nas the \"older — not the target style\".\n\n- **Human (`todos`)** follows the preferred shape exactly:\n  `Todos.svelte.ts:62-72` injects `auth`, `apiService` and `localStorage` with\n  defaults that preserve `new Todos()`.\n- **AI (`time-tracking`)** uses the older field-initialiser shape:\n  `TimeTracking.svelte.ts:26-31` declares `auth = getAuth();` and assigns\n  `this.apiService = this.auth.apiService` in the constructor. It defaults to\n  the shared `auth.apiService` (good), but it cannot be overridden by a\n  constructor argument and forces tests to mock the `Auth` module.\n\nThis is the one place where the human is *directly* the textbook example the\npolicy cites, and the AI is the cited anti-pattern.\n\n### 2.2 Member ordering\n\n`docs/STATE_SERVICES.md` §\"Member ordering\" says `Todos` is the reference order:\nconstants → `$state` fields → collaborators → constructor → public methods →\n`Symbol` + `setX`/`getX` at the bottom.\n\n- Both stores follow the order well. `TimeTracking.svelte.ts` has no `static\n  readonly` constants, no `Symbol`-adjacent deviations, and puts\n  `setTimeTracking`/`getTimeTracking` at the bottom (`:163-171`) — compliant.\n- The AI's store is noticeably **flatter and more consistent** in its CRUD\n  bodies: every mutator is `const res = await …; if (res) await this.load…;\n  return res;` (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:60-153`). The human's store mixes\n  `return res;`, `return ok;` and `return Promise.resolve(res !== null);` in the\n  same file (`Todos.svelte.ts:280-317` vs `:331-359`), including\n  `return Promise.resolve(...)` on already-`async` methods — redundant wrapping\n  that the AI never does.\n\n### 2.3 Delegation to pure services\n\n`docs/STATE_SERVICES.md` §\"Delegate pure logic to `services/`\" calls out `Todos`\nas the model: it holds four service instances as fields\n(`Todos.svelte.ts:57-60`) and delegates filtering, sorting, grouping and scoring.\n\n- **Human:** 4 single-purpose services (`TodoFilterService`,\n  `TodoSortingService`, `TodoGroupingService`, `TodoRelevanceService`), each\n  `new`-able, pure, and unit-tested.\n- **AI:** 1 service (`TimeTrackingGroupingService`) with a good \"why\" docblock\n  (`:13-17`). But other pure logic that the policy says should live in a service\n  is left inline in components:\n  - **`groupByDate` is duplicated verbatim** in\n    `GroupedEntries.svelte:18-30` and `routes/time-tracking/[id]/+page.svelte:67-79`.\n  - Week/today boundary math and aggregation live inline inside `$derived`\n    closures in `DashboardView.svelte:24-46`.\n  - `formatDuration` — pure string formatting — lives on the store\n    (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:155-160`), which `docs/DATA_LAYER.md` would place\n    in `helpers/` (there is a `NumberHelper`/`FormatHelper` family for this).\n\nThe AI clearly *can* extract a service (it did, and tested it well); it just\ndoesn't do so as consistently as the human.\n\n### 2.4 `load()` + `loaded` and error handling\n\n`docs/STATE_SERVICES.md` §\"The `load()` + `loaded` convention\" wants sub-loads\nvia `Promise.all` and a single `if (res)` assignment; `docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md`\n§\"Early returns\" and the general robustness rules favour explicit handling.\n\n- **Human:** `Todos.load()` (`:74-97`) sequences named steps and wraps the\n  parallel sub-loads in `try { … } finally { this.loadHideIt(); }` (`:92-96`),\n  and `loadWorkspaces` has a retry parameter (`:107-118`). Defensive,\n  failure-aware, and the reason its store test file is 367 lines.\n- **AI:** `TimeTracking.load()` (`:33-40`) is a bare `await Promise.all([…])`\n  followed by `this.loaded = true` — no `try`/`catch`/`finally`, no retry, no\n  guard if one sub-load rejects. Simpler and readable, but less robust.\n\n### 2.5 UI state on the store\n\n`docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"Local UI state via `$state`\" says \"UI-only state stays in\nthe component; anything the route, another component or the backend cares about\nbelongs on a store.\"\n\n- **Human:** the store carries transient UI flags — `quickCreateOpen`\n  (`Todos.svelte.ts:53`, written by `TodoCreate.svelte:62,73` and read by\n  `ViewSwitcher.svelte:10`) and `recentlyCreated` (`:52`, a 10-second highlight\n  set by `quickCreate`). `sortByScore`/`hideItActive` on the store are defensible\n  (shared/persisted), but `quickCreateOpen` is arguably a component concern.\n- **AI:** keeps every modal/drawer flag in the component that owns it —\n  `showEntryCreate` (`routes/time-tracking/+page.svelte:13`), `editModal` /\n  `deleteModal` / `editingEntry` (`[id]/+page.svelte:32-35`),\n  `showProjectCreate` (`+layout.svelte:15`). Cleaner separation.\n\nHere the AI is the more policy-compliant one.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Layout vs. page responsibilities\n\n`docs/DATA_LAYER.md` says each feature layout \"creates its feature stores **and\ncalls `load()` on them**\".\n\n- **Human:** `routes/todos/+layout.svelte:1-11` is minimal — it only calls\n  `setTodos()`/`setTags()`; the actual bootstrap (`onMount` → `todos.load()`,\n  viewport defaulting, `tags.load()`) lives in `routes/todos/+page.svelte:28-37`.\n- **AI:** `routes/time-tracking/+layout.svelte:10-27` does\n  `setTimeTracking()`, `onMount` → `tt.load()`, and gates the whole shell behind\n  `{#if tt.loaded}` — it also owns the navigation and the create/edit project\n  modal orchestration. This matches the documented \"layout bootstraps the\n  feature\" pattern more literally.\n\nTwo corollaries:\n\n1. **Single responsibility of the layout.** The AI's layout is a full shell\n   (nav + load + 2 modals + gate); the human's is a one-liner. The human's\n   layout is *simpler*, the AI's is *closer to the documented intent*. Both are\n   defensible; they are simply different axes.\n2. **Nested route.** Time-tracking has a real detail route\n   (`[id]/+page.svelte`, 205 LOC) and derives the selected project from\n   `page.params.id` (`+layout.svelte:18`, `[id]/+page.svelte:37-39`). Todos has\n   no detail route — selection is pure store state (`activeWorkspace`,\n   `activeFilters`). The AI's route-driven selection survived reloads and deep\n   links; the human's filter state is rebuilt from `page.url.searchParams` in\n   `checkUrlForFilter()` (`Todos.svelte.ts:148-159`).\n\n---\n\n## 4. View / component decomposition\n\n- **Human (`todos`)** decomposes along *view mode* and *prop*: four view\n  variants (`views/{ListView,CardView,OverviewView,KanbanView}`), a per-field\n  `props/` folder (15 tiny components: `Title`, `Category`, `Priority`, …), an\n  `actions/` folder (`TodoCreate`, `TodoDelete`, `HideIt`, `SortByScore`) and a\n  desktop/mobile navigation split (`TodoNavigationDesktop` 149 LOC /\n  `TodoNavigationMobile` 216 LOC). Components are small and single-purpose, at\n  the cost of 35 files and some duplication (`getBgColor` is copy-pasted in\n  `Card.svelte:31-40` and `CompactCard.svelte:15-24` — allowed by the \"three or\n  more times\" DRY rule).\n- **AI (`time-tracking`)** decomposes along *screen/role*: one navigation\n  component (`TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte`, 363 LOC — the largest file in\n  either feature), one entry component, one entry editor, one project editor,\n  and one component per dashboard widget. Views are selected by a URL `?tab=`\n  param (`routes/time-tracking/+page.svelte:15-19`,\n  `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:31-35`) rather than a component switcher.\n\nThe AI's `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte` concentrates project list, category\nCRUD, a colour picker, inline rename, two delete-confirmation modals and the\nmobile drawer in one 363-line component. `docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"Keep the\n`<script>` readable … If it stops being scannable, split components\" suggests\nthe human's instinct (split by concern) is the documented one, though the AI\nkeeps the file logically sectioned with snippets.\n\n**Dead code:** `PomodoroWidget.svelte` (211 LOC) is imported nowhere in `src/`\n(verified with `grep -rl`). It is the single largest dead-code artifact found in\neither feature. The human has no equivalent (the one candidate,\n`FindCategoryMenu.svelte`, is used by `settings/WorkspaceEdit.svelte`).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Reactivity patterns (`$derived` discipline)\n\n`docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md` §\"`$derived` discipline\" is explicit: use `$derived`\nonly for genuine, side-effect-free derivations read while rendering, and move\n`filter().map().sort()` chains into a method or service.\n\n- **Human:** modest use of `$derived` (7 files, one or two per file), and the one\n  aggregation is `OverviewView.svelte:19-42` using the correct **`$derived.by`**\n  form. Filtering/sorting/grouping is never a `$derived` — it is `Todos.useFilters`,\n  a method (`Todos.svelte.ts:161-196`), exactly as the policy prescribes.\n- **AI:** two genuine deviations:\n  1. **`$derived(() => …)` misuse** in `DashboardView.svelte:24,35,37,46` and\n     `StatisticsView.svelte:52`. In Svelte 5 this makes the derived value *a\n     function*, forcing call sites like `weekBreakdown()` (`:58,63`) and\n     `categoryBreakdown()` (`:66,67`) to invoke it. It happens to work, but it\n     is the classic runes footgun; the correct form is `$derived.by(...)` — which\n     the *same AI* uses correctly elsewhere (`EntryCreate.svelte:85`,\n     `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:31`, `routes/time-tracking/+page.svelte:15`).\n     Inconsistent within a single feature.\n  2. **Aggregation chains inside `$derived`** (`StatisticsView.svelte:52-64`\n     builds a `Map`, sums minutes and sorts — precisely the \"`filter().map().sort()`\n     hidden behind `$derived`\" the policy says to extract).\n\n- **Reactive time:** the human uses `SvelteDate` from `svelte/reactivity` in the\n  filter pipeline (`Todos.svelte.ts:165-171`) so the \"auto-generated due within\n  3 days\" window stays reactive; the AI uses plain `new Date()` inside\n  `$derived` (`DashboardView.svelte:25,38`), which will not re-evaluate when the\n  clock moves.\n\n`routes/todos/+page.svelte:26` has a harmless redundant alias\n(`let view = $derived(todos.view)`), but it is not a violation.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Loading-indicator discipline\n\n`docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"Loading feedback\": \"Always `start()` before the await and\n`stop()` on **every** exit path.\"\n\n- **AI (`time-tracking`)** is disciplined. `EntryCreate.onSave`\n  (`:118-141`), `ProjectCreate.onSave` (`:47-68`), `TimerWidget.startTimer`\n  (`:24-34`) all check validity *before* `loadingIndicator.start()`, or stop on\n  every path. No leak was found.\n- **Human (`todos`)** has several early returns *after* `start()` with no\n  `stop()`, leaking the global spinner:\n  - `props/Title.svelte:54-65` — `onsubmit()` calls `start()`, then\n    `if (todoTitle === '') return;` (`:59-61`) without stopping.\n  - `props/Description.svelte:88-99` — same shape at `:93-95`.\n  - `props/Subtask.svelte:61-72` — same shape at `:66-68`.\n  - `ListAndAdd.svelte:57-76` — `start()` then `if (value.trim() === '') return;`\n    (`:60-62`).\n  - (`props/Category.svelte:59-90` gets this right by calling `stop()` before its\n    empty-title return — showing the human knows the rule, just doesn't apply it\n    uniformly.)\n\nThis is the clearest instance of the AI's mechanical consistency beating the\nhuman's hand-written inconsistency.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Error handling\n\n- **Human:** real error surfaces. `Todos.loadWorkspaces` retries once\n  (`:107-118`); `Category.svelte:78-86` and `Tags.svelte:73-83` call\n  `notifications.error(ts.get.todos.category_error / tag_error)` when a create\n  fails; `quickCreate` returns a discriminated `{ ok, recurrenceIgnored }`\n  result (`Todos.svelte.ts:249-278`) that `TodoCreate.svelte:86-96` turns into\n  success/warning notifications.\n- **AI:** close to fire-and-forget. Store mutators discard results and reload\n  (`TimeTracking.svelte.ts:60-153`); the only user feedback is the modal closing\n  unconditionally in `EntryCreate.onSave:133-140` / `ProjectCreate.onSave:52-67`,\n  even if the API call failed. There are no `notifications.error(...)` calls in\n  the time-tracking feature (verified by grep).\n\nThe human treats the API as fallible; the AI treats \"await returns\" as success.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Comments\n\n`docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md` §\"Comments explain why, not what\" cites\n`Todos.svelte.ts` as the reference (the `// Restore synchronously …` and\n`// Always re-apply hide-it …` blocks) and says not to leave commented-out code.\n\nThe current code contradicts the doc in an interesting way:\n\n- **`Todos.svelte.ts` contains zero comments.** The two comments the policy\n  quotes were introduced in `d7d4d92` and removed in `a34275e` (`git log -S`);\n  the reference example is now stale. All 35 todos components and both routes\n  also contain **no `//` comments at all**; `ScoreBar.svelte:29` even carries a\n  commented-out fragment (`<!-- left[-56px] left-2px -->`), which the policy\n  explicitly forbids.\n- **The AI's code is where the \"why\" comments actually are**: 8 comment lines in\n  the feature, e.g. `EntryCreate.svelte:70` (\"No silent default to the first\n  project\"), `:109-111` (why duration-only entries anchor to local noon),\n  `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:52-53` (why a concrete hex default for the\n  colour picker), and the JSDoc block in `TimeTrackingGroupingService.ts:13-17`\n  (the \"entries without a project are ignored\" contract).\n\nSo the *human-authored policy* is better honoured by the *AI-authored code* on\nthis axis. The human's comments migrated into the store tests instead, where\nthey are genuinely useful (see §11).\n\n---\n\n## 9. i18n, accessibility and shared primitives\n\n### i18n\n\n`docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"i18n — `ts.get.*`\": \"Never hardcode a UI string in a\ncomponent.\"\n\n- **AI (`time-tracking`)** is consistently translated: every visible string in\n  every component goes through `ts.get.timeTracking.*` (grep for bare English\n  literals in the feature returns nothing beyond the `—` placeholder and `×`).\n- **Human (`todos`)** has real deviations:\n  - `Counter.svelte:8` — literal `{todos.length} Todos` (its own test asserts\n    `'2 Todos'`).\n  - `KanbanView.svelte:27-40` — `'Backlog'`, `'In Progress'`, `'Waiting'`,\n    `'Almost done'`, `'Pending'` hardcoded in `getStatusLabel`.\n  - `Recurrence.svelte:116,125,144,150` — `Every`, `occurrence(s)`, `Cancel`,\n    `Save` hardcoded.\n  - `TodoCreate.svelte:121,131,135,139,143,147,159` — placeholder `Enter todo`\n    and the legend `Category / Tag / Due Date / Recurrence / Link`.\n\nThis is a striking reversal of the common assumption that AI output is the one\nlittered with hardcoded strings.\n\n### Accessibility\n\n`docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"Accessibility\" requires keyboard-reachable clickables and\nvisible focus.\n\n- **AI:** uses native `<button>` pervasively and gives icon-only controls\n  accessible names (`aria-label={ts.get.timeTracking.*}` in\n  `TimeTrackingNavigation.svelte:184,212,224,245,300,308` and\n  `ProjectCreate.svelte:102,113`).\n- **Human:** relies on `role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"` on non-native elements in 6\n  places, but **no todos component uses `aria-label` at all** — several icon-only\n  buttons (e.g. the remove-category button `Category.svelte:121-126`, the\n  trash button `DueDate.svelte:51-59`) are unlabelled.\n\n### Shared primitives\n\nBoth reuse the shared `ui/` and `forms/` primitives, but the human hand-rolls\nmore raw inputs where a primitive exists: `Title.svelte:74-82`,\n`Link.svelte:80-87` and `Recurrence.svelte:117-123,131-136` use\n`<input type=\"text\">`/`<input type=\"number\">` with bespoke classes instead of\n`forms/TextInput`/`NumberInput`, which `docs/COMPONENTS.md` §\"Prefer the shared\nprimitives\" calls a smell. The AI's forms are assembled from `ContentModal`,\n`TextInput`, `NumberInput`, `DateInput`, `DateTimeInput`, `Select` and\n`TextButton` throughout (`EntryCreate.svelte:144-208`, `ProjectCreate.svelte:71-145`).\n\n---\n\n## 10. Types\n\n- Both mirror the API in snake_case and keep type-only imports; both are\n  policy-compliant.\n- **Human:** 133 LOC with a rich set of request/response types\n  (`CreateTodoRequest`, `UpdateTodoRequest`, `TodoWorkspace`, `TodoSubtask`, …)\n  and filter unions (`TodoFilterType`, `TodoDueAtFilter`). Every declared type is\n  used.\n- **AI:** 97 LOC. The archived verdict already flagged one **dead type**,\n  `TimeTrackingStatisticsRequest` (`time_tracking.ts:74-78`), never referenced by\n  `loadStatistics` (which takes inline `from`/`to`/`categoryIds` params). A small\n  \"declared for completeness, never wired up\" habit.\n\nAlso note `TimeTracking.loadStatistics` (`:146-153`) **builds a query string by\nhand** (`apiRoutes.… + '?' + params.join('&')`). `docs/DATA_LAYER.md` says all\nendpoints live in `apiRoutes` and callers should never build URL strings. Query\nparams aren't modelled in `apiRoutes`, so this is a minor, arguably unavoidable\ndeviation — but it is still URL construction outside the config layer.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Tests\n\n`docs/TESTING.md` names `Todos` as the Priority-1 store (highest logic density)\nand both `TodoFilterService` and `TimeTrackingGroupingService` as covered pure\nlogic.\n\n- **Human (`todos`)** invests heavily in tests:\n  - Store: `tests/unit/stores/Todos.test.ts` — **367 lines**, covering\n    `useFilters` (backlog, hide-it, category/tag, auto-generated visibility),\n    URL filters, `quickCreate` recurrence-ignore, sort-by-score, hide-it\n    persistence/restore, and concurrency (\"respects a toggle issued while `load()`\n    is in flight\", `:330-365`).\n  - Services: `TodoFilterService.test.ts` (280), `TodoSortingService.test.ts`\n    (116), `TodoRelevanceService.test.ts` (96), `TodoGroupingService.test.ts` (48)\n    — ~540 lines.\n  - Components: `todos/Counter` is tested twice\n    (`tests/components/counter.test.ts`, `context-harness.test.ts`) and `NoTodos`\n    via `logic-leaves.test.ts`.\n  - The tests carry **explanatory \"why\" comments** — e.g. the regression note at\n    `Todos.test.ts:111-115` explaining a stale-filter bug. This is the human\n    comment style relocated into the test suite, where it is arguably most\n    valuable.\n- **AI (`time-tracking`)** tests the extracted service well but the feature\n  thinly:\n  - `tests/unit/services/TimeTrackingGroupingService.test.ts` — 96 lines, 5\n    cases including \"own colour, not first category\", fallback colour, ignores\n    project-less entries and no input mutation. Good coverage of the service.\n  - `tests/unit/stores/TimeTracking.test.ts` — only 64 lines: `load()`,\n    no-active-timer, one `startTimer`, and `formatDuration` cases. The store's\n    other mutators are untested (defensible — `docs/TESTING.md` §8 says not to\n    test boilerplate CRUD — but `loadStatistics` param building, which *is*\n    non-trivial, has no test).\n  - **No component tests** for any time-tracking widget, despite\n    `TimeBreakdownWidget` being a clean props-driven leaf (the exact shape\n    `docs/TESTING.md` §8 Priority-2 says to test).\n\nTest : source ratio is roughly **0.24 (human) vs. 0.075 (AI)**.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Commit granularity\n\nNot code style, but part of the observed behaviour:\n\n- **Human:** `todos` shows ~33 commits over months, including small,\n  intent-named fixes (`hide it should apply to backlog`,\n  `hide it doesnt always reload`, `Auto-create categories in Todos if\n  non-existent`). The feature history reads as a sequence of discovered edge\n  cases.\n- **AI:** `time-tracking`'s rework landed as one 33-file, +2441/−579 commit\n  (`b25801c`) plus a small visual follow-up (`48cea28`). Wide, coherent, and\n  decisively big — the opposite of incremental.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Style-characteristics table\n\nLegend: ✅ matches the repo's documented convention · ⚠️ deviates.\n\n| Convention (source doc)                                     | todos (human) | time-tracking (AI) |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------ |\n| Constructor DI (injected collaborators) — STATE_SERVICES     | ✅ reference  | ⚠️ field-init      |\n| One class/file, `setX`/`getX`, member order — STATE_SERVICES | ✅            | ✅                 |\n| `load()` + `loaded`, parallel sub-loads — STATE_SERVICES     | ✅ (+retry/`finally`) | ✅ (no error handling) |\n| Delegate pure logic to `services/` — STATE_SERVICES/DATA     | ✅ 4 services | ⚠️ 1 service; inline `groupByDate`×2, `formatDuration` on store |\n| Return semantics (`res`/`ok`, no redundant `Promise.resolve`) — STATE_SERVICES | ⚠️ mixed | ✅ consistent |\n| UI-only state stays in component — COMPONENTS                | ⚠️ `quickCreateOpen`, `recentlyCreated` on store | ✅ |\n| `$derived` discipline (`$derived.by`, no chains) — BEST_PRACTICES | ✅ `$derived.by` for aggregation | ⚠️ `$derived(() => …)` ×5, aggregation in derived |\n| Comments explain why; no commented-out code — BEST_PRACTICES | ⚠️ none; one commented-out fragment | ✅ 8 why-comments (incl. service docblock) |\n| Loading indicator stopped on every path — COMPONENTS         | ⚠️ 4 early-return leaks | ✅ |\n| Error handling / user feedback — BEST_PRACTICES              | ✅ retry + notifications | ⚠️ fire-and-forget |\n| i18n: no hardcoded UI strings — COMPONENTS/DATA              | ⚠️ Counter, Kanban labels, Recurrence, TodoCreate | ✅ fully translated |\n| Accessibility: labels + native elements — COMPONENTS         | ⚠️ no `aria-label`; `role=\"button\"` fallbacks | ✅ `aria-label` on icon buttons, native `<button>` |\n| Shared primitives — COMPONENTS                               | ⚠️ hand-rolled inputs | ✅ primitives everywhere |\n| Types: no dead types, no hand-built URLs — DATA              | ✅ | ⚠️ dead `TimeTrackingStatisticsRequest`; hand-built query string |\n| Tests for logic/contracts — TESTING                          | ✅ deep store + 4 service suites + component tests | ⚠️ good service test, thin store test, no component tests |\n| No dead components                                            | ✅ | ⚠️ `PomodoroWidget` (211 LOC) unused |\n\nScore (informational): both authors are **9 ✅ / 7 ⚠️**. The counts are\nidentical, but they land on *different* rules — which is the whole story.\n\n---\n\n## 14. What the human does that the AI doesn't\n\n1. **Evolves in small, narrative commits.** `todos` history reads as a chain of\n   specific fixes; the AI lands a feature in one wide change.\n2. **Extracts pure logic into multiple dedicated services** (`Todo*Service`)\n   before it is strictly necessary, and unit-tests each exhaustively.\n3. **Bakes in defensive behaviour**: retry on a failed sub-load, `try/finally`\n   so a post-load step always runs, discriminated results\n   (`{ ok, recurrenceIgnored }`) instead of bare booleans.\n4. **Surfaces API failures to the user** via `UiNotifications` error messages\n   and treats create/update as fallible.\n5. **Writes the reference DI store** the policy quotes verbatim.\n6. **Runs a much deeper store test suite** (367 lines, incl. concurrency and\n   regression cases) and keeps test rationale in comments.\n7. **Uses `SvelteDate`** to keep time-window derivation reactive.\n8. **Splits navigation by viewport** into dedicated desktop/mobile components.\n\n## 15. What the AI does that the human doesn't\n\n1. **Mechanical consistency in CRUD bodies** — one return shape, no redundant\n   `Promise.resolve` wrapping.\n2. **Stops the loading indicator on every exit path** (the human leaks it four\n   times).\n3. **Translates every UI string** and **labels every icon-only button**; uses\n   native `<button>` over `role=\"button\"` divs.\n4. **Assembles forms from shared primitives** (`ContentModal`, `TextInput`,\n   `NumberInput`, `DateInput`, `DateTimeInput`, `Select`) instead of hand-rolled\n   inputs.\n5. **Writes \"why\" comments** at the non-obvious points (colour defaults, noon\n   anchoring, contract assumptions) — the very behaviour the policy documents\n   but the human's current code no longer exhibits.\n6. **Keeps transient UI state in components**, not on the store.\n7. **Drives view selection from the URL** (`?tab=`, `[id]` route), so reload /\n   deep-link / back-forward work.\n8. **Uses `$derived.by`** where the derivation is a block (and documents pure\n   contracts with JSDoc on the service).\n\n## 16. Where each author deviates from the repo's own conventions\n\n- **Human deviations:** i18n gaps (`Counter`, `KanbanView`, `Recurrence`,\n  `TodoCreate`); four spinner leaks; no `aria-label`; hand-rolled inputs;\n  mixed return semantics; a commented-out markup fragment; UI flags on the store;\n  (and, ironically, the absence of the comments the policy attributes to it).\n  Note the policy docs are themselves human-authored, so these are the author\n  deviating from his own written rules.\n- **AI deviations:** the older field-initialiser store shape the docs mark\n  \"not the target style\"; `$derived(() => …)` misuse (inconsistent with its own\n  correct `$derived.by` usage); pure logic left inline in components; 211 LOC of\n  dead component code; a dead type; a hand-built query string; no error feedback\n  on failed mutations; and a thinner test footprint for the feature's own UI.\n\nNeither style is uniformly \"clean\". The human optimises for **robustness and\nincremental understanding**; the AI optimises for **local consistency and\nconvention coverage**.\n\n---\n\n## 17. Summary — how the styles differ\n\nThe human writes like someone who has been burned by the failures the code will\neventually hit. His store is the policy's reference implementation: injected\ndependencies, pure logic pushed into four tested services, retries and\n`finally` blocks, user-visible errors, and a deep regression-oriented test\nsuite. Its weaknesses are the classic ones of hand-written code — drift over\ntime (stale doc comments, four leaked spinners, hardcoded strings in the\nolder components, hand-rolled inputs) and a tendency to keep UI flags on the\nstore.\n\nThe AI writes like a diligent implementer of the written rules, applied locally\nand uniformly, without the accumulated wear of real defects. Its CRUD shape,\nloading discipline, i18n coverage, accessibility labelling and use of shared\nprimitives are consistently better *within each file* than the human's. Its\nweaknesses are the classic ones of generated code — a few known runes\nfootguns (`$derived(() => …)`), logic that should have been extracted left\ninline, an unused 211-line widget, a dead type, an unused robustness gap\n(no error handling), and a thinner, more \"happy-path\" test footprint.\n\nPut plainly: **the human's code knows failure modes; the AI's code knows the\nstyle guide.** The gap that matters for this repo is not raw capability — both\nproduce working, readable code — but *where the effort is spent*: the human\nspends it on edge cases and structure, the AI on uniformity and surface\nconventions. The policy documents (`docs/BEST_PRACTICES.md` et al.) are, on\nbalance, followed at least as well by the AI on the axes they measure (i18n,\na11y, primitives, loading discipline, comments), and better followed by the\nhuman on the structural and robustness axes (DI, service extraction, error\nhandling, tests, incremental change).\n\nA practical reading for future work: the AI's habits are excellent for the\nshallow, greppable rules and should be kept; the human's habits are the ones to\ncopy for the structural, defensive, and testing decisions that the written\npolicy only gestures at.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":574,"totalLines":574,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/coding-style.md","time":{"start":1791728734240,"end":1791728734281}},"id":"prt_12b5acfb2001clFi3c0785n8lZ","sessionID":"ses_ed4a56c6fffevAGmwB1j5o7wcj","messageID":"msg_12b5ac5ec001MN1zGiYJvvnReN"}}
